I18N.manual = "          <div id=\"help-start\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>🚀 Getting started</h2>\n            <p>Follow this order the first time. Each item shows whether it is already done:</p>\n            <ol class=\"space-y-2\">\n              <li class=\"flex gap-2\"><span x-text=\"router.online ? '✅' : '⬜'\"></span><span><b>Connect to the MikroTik</b> — the badge at the top should read <span class=\"badge b-green\">MikroTik online</span>. If it doesn't, see <a @click=\"scrollHelp('problems')\">Troubleshooting</a>.</span></li>\n              <li class=\"flex gap-2\"><span x-text=\"overview?.vpn.configured ? '✅' : '⬜'\"></span><span><b>Create (or adopt) the WireGuard interface</b> — in <a @click=\"tab='vpn'\">WireGuard VPN</a> → Start setup. If the MikroTik already has WireGuard (e.g. reinstalled panel), use <b>Adopt and import clients</b>.</span></li>\n              <li class=\"flex gap-2\"><span x-text=\"overview?.vpn.total ? '✅' : '⬜'\"></span><span><b>Add the first device</b> — WireGuard VPN → ＋ New device → scan the QR Code in the WireGuard app.</span></li>\n              <li class=\"flex gap-2\"><span>⬜</span><span><b>Turn on alerts</b> — in <a @click=\"tab='alerts'\">Alerts</a>, set up Discord and/or Telegram and click \"Save and send test\".</span></li>\n              <li class=\"flex gap-2\"><span>⬜</span><span><b>Make a backup</b> — in <a @click=\"tab='backup'\">Backup</a> → Download backup, and keep it somewhere safe.</span></li>\n              <li class=\"flex gap-2\"><span>⬜</span><span><b>Change the admin password</b> — side menu → 🔑 Change password.</span></li>\n              <li class=\"flex gap-2\"><span>⬜</span><span><b>Make sure the panel is not on the internet</b> — follow <a @click=\"scrollHelp('security')\">🔒 Security</a> and run the 4G test.</span></li>\n            </ol>\n            <p class=\"hint\">Optional: <a @click=\"scrollHelp('site')\">connect clients' MikroTiks</a> to yours (site-to-site) and <a @click=\"scrollHelp('routers')\">manage them from the panel</a>, and <a @click=\"tab='nat'\">open ports</a> for game servers/applications.</p>\n            <p class=\"hint\">Managing more than one MikroTik? Everything in this list applies to the router chosen in the <b>selector at the top</b>.</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-security\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>🔒 Security</h2>\n            <div class=\"text-sm text-red-200 bg-red-950/40 border border-red-900 rounded-lg p-3\">\n              <b>⚠️ NEVER expose the panel to the internet.</b> It has configuration access to your MikroTiks. Use it <b>only from the local network or through the WireGuard VPN</b> — to use it away from home, connect to the VPN first and then open the panel.</div>\n            <h3>1. Do not create a port forward to the panel</h3>\n            <p>The panel is only exposed if there is a port forward pointing to it. <b>Never</b> open the panel's port (8080) on the \"Port forwarding\" screen or in Winbox. Check in the MikroTik terminal — both lists must come back empty:</p>\n            <pre class=\"mono text-xs bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800 rounded-lg p-3 overflow-x-auto\">/ip firewall nat print where dst-port=8080\n/ip firewall nat print where to-ports=8080</pre>\n            <h3>2. Panel on Docker (PC/server)</h3>\n            <ul>\n              <li>In <span class=\"mono\">docker-compose.yml</span>, publish the port only on the LAN IP: <span class=\"mono\">\"192.168.88.61:8080:8080\"</span> (use the machine's IP) and run <span class=\"mono\">docker compose up -d</span>.</li>\n              <li>Windows (PowerShell as Administrator) — allows the port only for the local network and the VPN:<br>\n                <span class=\"mono text-xs\">New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName \"WG Panel (LAN and VPN only)\" -Direction Inbound -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 8080 -RemoteAddress LocalSubnet,10.10.0.0/24 -Action Allow</span></li>\n              <li>Linux: Docker bypasses ufw for published ports — the previous item (publish only on the LAN IP) is what protects you. See SECURITY.md for the <span class=\"mono\">DOCKER-USER</span> chain.</li>\n            </ul>\n            <h3>3. Panel as a container on the MikroTik</h3>\n            <p>Paste into the MikroTik terminal (adjust your network and VPN ranges). Only the local network and the VPN reach the panel:</p>\n            <pre class=\"mono text-xs bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800 rounded-lg p-3 overflow-x-auto\">/ip firewall address-list add list=wgpanel-acesso address=192.168.88.0/24 comment=\"WG Panel: local network\"\n/ip firewall address-list add list=wgpanel-acesso address=10.10.0.0/24 comment=\"WG Panel: VPN\"\n/ip firewall filter add chain=forward action=drop protocol=tcp dst-address=172.20.0.2 dst-port=8080 src-address-list=!wgpanel-acesso comment=\"WG Panel: panel only for LAN and VPN\" place-before=[:pick [/ip firewall filter find where dynamic=no] 0]</pre>\n            <h3>4. Test</h3>\n            <ol class=\"list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1\">\n              <li>On your phone, turn off Wi-Fi and the VPN (4G only) and open <span class=\"mono\">http://YOUR-DDNS:8080</span> — <b>it must not open</b>.</li>\n              <li>Turn on the VPN and open the panel's internal IP — <b>it must open</b>.</li>\n            </ol>\n            <p>If item 1 opens the panel, it is exposed: review your port forwards immediately.</p>\n            <h3>Other good practices</h3>\n            <ul>\n              <li>A strong password for the panel's <span class=\"mono\">admin</span>, and \"Read-only\" users for people who only watch.</li>\n              <li>On the MikroTik, the panel uses the limited <span class=\"mono\">wgpanel</span> user — <b>never</b> the admin. Restrict the API (IP → Services) to the networks the panel connects from.</li>\n              <li>Keep <span class=\"mono\">.env</span>, the <span class=\"mono\">data/</span> folder and backups safe: they contain passwords and the VPN keys.</li>\n            </ul>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-dashboard\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>📊 Dashboard</h2>\n            <p>Overview of what is happening right now on the <b>router chosen in the selector at the top</b>. It refreshes by itself every 15 seconds (the <b>⟳</b> button forces an immediate collection).</p>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b>Your routers</b> — shows up when the panel manages more than one MikroTik: 🟢/🔴 for each, model, CPU and uptime. Click one to switch.</li>\n              <li><b>Router</b> — model, RouterOS version and how long it has been up.</li>\n              <li><b>CPU / Memory</b> — current usage. High CPU for a long time can slow down the internet.</li>\n              <li><b>WireGuard VPN</b> — how many clients (devices and remote MikroTiks) of this router are connected now, across all its interfaces.</li>\n              <li><b>Forwarded ports</b> — how many forwards are active and whether the servers are responding.</li>\n              <li><b>Internet traffic</b> — download/upload of your link. The internet interface is <b>detected automatically</b> from the default route (e.g. <span class=\"mono\">pppoe-out1</span>); its name shows below the chart.</li>\n              <li><b>Recent events</b> — latest happenings: someone connected, a server went down, a port expired…</li>\n            </ul>\n            <button class=\"btn\" @click=\"tab='dashboard'\">Go to the Dashboard →</button>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-routers\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>🖧 Routers (several MikroTiks)</h2>\n            <p>The panel manages <b>your MikroTik</b> (the \"main\" one, from the <span class=\"mono\">.env</span> file) and also <b>your clients' MikroTiks</b>. The <b>selector at the top</b> chooses which one you are viewing: VPN, ports, charts and settings switch to that router.</p>\n            <h3>How to add a client's MikroTik (recommended)</h3>\n            <ol class=\"list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1\">\n              <li>In <b>VPN → 🖧 Other MikroTik</b>, create the tunnel with <b>\"Manage this MikroTik from the panel\"</b> turned on (default).</li>\n              <li>Paste the script into the client's MikroTik terminal. Besides the tunnel, it creates the <span class=\"mono\">wgpanel</span> user with limited permissions, a random password and <b>login only through the tunnel</b>, and enables the API.</li>\n              <li>In a moment the router shows up in <b>Routers</b> and in the selector at the top.</li>\n            </ol>\n            <p>Tunnels created before this option: use 🛠️ on the client's row (VPN) — the panel generates only the management snippet.</p>\n            <h3>Why a limited user (and not the Winbox admin)?</h3>\n            <p>If the panel were hacked, admin passwords would give full control over your clients' routers. The <span class=\"mono\">wgpanel</span> user cannot change passwords or users and <b>can only log in through the tunnel</b> — it does not work over the internet.</p>\n            <h3>Terminal</h3>\n            <p>For commands, use <b>🌐 WebFig → Terminal</b>. The panel deliberately has no terminal of its own: that would require giving it full access to the routers, and the <span class=\"mono\">wgpanel</span> user is limited precisely so that, if the panel were hacked, nobody could control your MikroTiks.</p>\n            <h3>On the Routers screen</h3>\n            <ul>\n              <li>🟢 / 🔴 status, model, version, CPU, memory and how many VPN clients and ports each one has. <span class=\"badge b-amber\">⬆️ RouterOS … available</span> shows up when there is an update (see <a @click=\"scrollHelp('seccheck')\">Security check</a>).</li>\n              <li><b>🔌 Test</b> — checks the connection; for routers behind a tunnel, it finds the right API port by itself.</li>\n              <li><b>🌐 WebFig</b> — opens the MikroTik's own page in a new tab (also at the top, next to the selector). There you log in with <b>your</b> user and get the <b>Terminal</b>, just like in Winbox. For clients, it works through the tunnel. If the browser warns about the certificate (self-signed https), that's expected: proceed.</li>\n              <li><b>✏️ Edit</b> — name, VPN addresses and (except the main one) URL, user and password.</li>\n              <li><b>🗑️</b> — removes the router from the panel (does not touch its configuration).</li>\n              <li><b>＋ Add manually</b> — for routers the panel can already reach without the script.</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p>Discord alerts say which router each notification is about (e.g. <span class=\"mono\">[Bakery] MikroTik unreachable</span>).</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-vpn\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>🔐 WireGuard VPN</h2>\n            <p>A VPN creates a secure \"tunnel\" to your network. With it you reach your home/office things from anywhere — cameras, NAS, servers, printers, the MikroTik itself — as if you were there.</p>\n            <p>The panel works with two kinds of client:</p>\n            <table class=\"table\">\n              <tr><td>📱 <b>Device</b></td><td>A single device (phone, laptop). Set it up by scanning a <b>QR Code</b> or importing a <span class=\"mono\">.conf</span> file.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>🖧 <b>Other MikroTik</b></td><td>A client's router and the whole network behind it. Set it up by pasting a <b>script</b> into their MikroTik terminal. See <a @click=\"scrollHelp('site')\">Other MikroTik</a>.</td></tr>\n            </table>\n\n            <h3>Interfaces (the tabs at the top of the screen)</h3>\n            <p>Each tab is a WireGuard interface on the MikroTik, with its own <b>port</b> and <b>IP range</b>. Use more than one to separate purposes — for example <span class=\"mono\">wg-home</span> for your devices and <span class=\"mono\">wg-clients</span> for clients' MikroTiks. The number on the tab shows <b>online / total</b>.</p>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b>Empty panel, MikroTik with WireGuard</b> (new or reinstalled panel) — the screen shows <b>\"This MikroTik already has WireGuard\"</b> with the <b>Adopt and import clients</b> button: it brings in the interface and opens the import; with the keys stored on the MikroTik, clients come with a QR Code. There is also a shortcut to restore a backup.</li>\n              <li><b>＋ New interface</b> — creates an interface (the panel suggests a free port and range) or <b>adopts</b> one that already exists on the MikroTik.</li>\n              <li><b>VPN range</b> — must be different from your network (your network is 192.168.88.x → use 10.10.0.x).</li>\n              <li><b>Networks here</b> — the networks of your home/office that clients can reach.</li>\n              <li><b>Treat as internal network (LAN)</b> — lets devices reach the network, DNS and the internet. For an interface used only by clients' MikroTiks, you can uncheck it.</li>\n              <li>⚙️ reconfigures · 🗑️ deletes (only when it has no clients; it can also remove it from the MikroTik).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h3>Devices: what goes through the VPN?</h3>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b>🏠 Local network only</b> — reaches your network; the device's internet stays as usual. Recommended in most cases.</li>\n              <li><b>🌐 All internet</b> — all browsing goes through your MikroTik. Useful on public Wi-Fi.</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p>When you change a device's mode, the new QR opens by itself: <b>scan it again</b> on the device (the mode is stored on it).</p>\n            <p><b>Remote × local access:</b> in the QR/file, use <b>🌍 remote</b> (public IP/DDNS) when the device is away from home and <b>🏠 local</b> (MikroTik's IP on the network) when inside it.</p>\n\n            <h3>Status</h3>\n            <table class=\"table\">\n              <tr><td><span class=\"badge b-green\">Online</span></td><td>Connected — there was traffic in the last few minutes.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><span class=\"badge b-amber\">Offline</span></td><td>Registered, but not connected right now. The drop alert takes ~3 min (WireGuard does not report when someone disconnects).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><span class=\"badge b-gray\">Disabled</span></td><td>You blocked access (⏸️). Enable it again with ▶️.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><span class=\"badge b-red\">Missing on MikroTik</span></td><td>Someone deleted the client directly in Winbox. Use 🔁 to recreate it, or 🗑️ to remove it for good (through the panel, which also cleans up routes and rules).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><span class=\"badge b-red\">Disabled on MikroTik</span></td><td>Active in the panel, but disabled on the router (e.g. changed in Winbox). Use 🔁 to fix it.</td></tr>\n            </table>\n\n            <h3>Buttons</h3>\n            <table class=\"table\">\n              <tr><td>📈</td><td>Usage chart.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>📱 / 📜</td><td>Device: QR Code and <span class=\"mono\">.conf</span> file. Other MikroTik: script to paste into the client's router.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>🖥️</td><td>Remote MikroTik already managed: opens its router in the panel (switches the selector at the top).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>🛠️</td><td>Remote MikroTik not managed yet: generates the script snippet that creates the limited user and enables the API, so it shows up in Routers.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>✏️</td><td>Edit name, owner, mode (device) or networks and access type (MikroTik).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>⏸️ / ▶️</td><td>Block / allow access without deleting.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>🔁</td><td>Shows up when the MikroTik differs from the panel: fixes it with one click.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>🔑</td><td>Generate new keys (e.g. lost phone). The old configuration stops working.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>🗑️</td><td>Remove the client for good.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>⬇️ Import from MikroTik</td><td>Brings in peers created outside the panel (Winbox, an old VPN) without changing anything on the router. Those marked <span class=\"badge b-green\">🔑 with key</span> come complete, with QR/script; the others show as <span class=\"badge b-gray\">imported</span> (monitored, but without QR). The peer's interface must be in the panel — adopt it first in ＋ New interface.</td></tr>\n            </table>\n            <p><b>Keys stored on the MikroTik:</b> with the option turned on in Settings (default), each client's key is also saved on the router. That way any new panel imports the clients <b>with working QR Codes</b>, without needing a backup.</p>\n\n            <h3>How to install on the device</h3>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b>Phone</b> — \"WireGuard\" app (Play Store / App Store) → <b>+</b> → Scan from QR code → turn on.</li>\n              <li><b>Windows/Mac/Linux</b> — install from wireguard.com/install → \"Import tunnel(s) from file\" → select the <span class=\"mono\">.conf</span> → Activate.</li>\n            </ul>\n            <button class=\"btn\" @click=\"tab='vpn'\">Go to VPN →</button>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-site\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>🖧 Other MikroTik (site-to-site)</h2>\n            <p>Connects a <b>client's</b> MikroTik to yours through a tunnel. From your home you open their router's Winbox and reach the devices on their network (cameras, computers), and the panel alerts you on Discord if the client goes down.</p>\n<pre class=\"mono text-xs bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800 rounded-lg p-3 overflow-x-auto leading-5\">   YOUR HOME                                CLIENT (e.g. Bakery)\n┌─────────────────┐                      ┌──────────────────────┐\n│ Your MikroTik   │◄════════ tunnel ════►│ Client's MikroTik    │\n│ 192.168.88.x    │  (over the internet) │ 192.168.10.x         │\n│  └ your PC      │                      │  ├ cameras           │\n│  └ panel        │                      │  └ computers         │\n└─────────────────┘                      └──────────────────────┘</pre>\n            <h3>Step by step</h3>\n            <ol class=\"list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1\">\n              <li><b>Only once:</b> create an interface for clients (<b>＋ New interface</b>, e.g. <span class=\"mono\">wg-clients</span>).</li>\n              <li>On its tab, click <b>🖧 Other MikroTik</b> and enter the name and the <b>client's local network</b> (see it in their Winbox under IP → Addresses, e.g. <span class=\"mono\">192.168.10.0/24</span>).</li>\n              <li>Choose <b>who accesses whom</b> (below) and keep <b>\"Manage this MikroTik from the panel\"</b> on if you want to create VPNs, ports and see charts of their router here (enter their WebFig port — default 80).</li>\n              <li>The panel shows a <b>script</b>. In the client's MikroTik Winbox → <b>New Terminal</b> → paste it and press Enter (only once). You can copy it or download the <span class=\"mono\">.rsc</span>.</li>\n              <li>In seconds it shows up <span class=\"badge b-green\">Online</span>. Reach their router through the <b>tunnel IP</b> (\"VPN IP\" column, e.g. 10.20.0.2) and their devices through the normal IP of their network (e.g. 192.168.10.50).</li>\n              <li>If you checked \"Manage\", it also shows up in <a @click=\"scrollHelp('routers')\">Routers</a> and in the selector at the top.</li>\n            </ol>\n            <h3>Who accesses whom?</h3>\n            <table class=\"table\">\n              <tr><td><span class=\"badge b-blue\">➡️ Only I access</span></td><td>You get into the client's network; the client <b>cannot</b> open connections to your network. Recommended and default. The panel creates a rule on your MikroTik that blocks only <b>new</b> connections coming from the client.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><span class=\"badge b-amber\">↔️ Two-way</span></td><td>Both sides reach each other. Use only if the client really needs something on your network.</td></tr>\n            </table>\n            <p>You can change it later with ✏️, without touching the client's MikroTik.</p>\n            <h3>What the panel does on each side</h3>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b>On your MikroTik:</b> the client's peer, routes to their network and (in \"only I access\" mode) the blocking rule.</li>\n              <li><b>On the client's MikroTik (through the script):</b> the <span class=\"mono\">wg-central</span> interface pointing to your DDNS, the tunnel IP, routes to your network and adding the tunnel to their LAN list (so you can get into Winbox).</li>\n              <li><b>With \"Manage\" on, also:</b> the <span class=\"mono\">wgpanel</span> user (limited group, random password, login <b>only</b> from the tunnel networks) and the www service enabled for the API. If their www is already in use with restrictions, the script tells you what to adjust.</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p class=\"text-amber-300\">⚠️ The two networks must be <b>different</b>. If the client also uses <span class=\"mono\">192.168.88.x</span> (MikroTik's factory default, same as yours), the panel refuses — change the client's network range first. The script contains the tunnel key: send it over a secure channel.</p>\n            <p>To redo it on the client (e.g. after 🔑 generating new keys): on their MikroTik, remove the <span class=\"mono\">wg-central</span> interface and run the new script.</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-nat\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>🎮 Port forwarding</h2>\n            <p>Also called <b>port forward</b> or <b>port redirection</b>. It lets people on the internet reach a service on your network — for example, friends joining your Minecraft server.</p>\n            <h3>Before opening a port</h3>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b>Pin the server's IP</b>: in Winbox, IP → DHCP Server → Leases → click the server → <b>Make Static</b>. If the IP changes, the forward stops working.</li>\n              <li>Also allow it in the <b>server's own firewall</b> (e.g. Windows Firewall).</li>\n            </ul>\n            <h3>Fields</h3>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b>Ready-made template</b> — fills in the right ports for well-known games and apps.</li>\n              <li><b>External port</b> — the port people use on the internet. Accepts <span class=\"mono\">25565</span>, a range <span class=\"mono\">2456-2458</span> or a list <span class=\"mono\">80,443</span>.</li>\n              <li><b>Server IP / Internal port</b> — where the traffic goes. Empty internal port = same as the external one.</li>\n              <li><b>Allow only these IPs</b> — only these addresses can connect. Great for playing only with friends or for RDP/SSH.</li>\n              <li><b>Close automatically</b> — the port closes by itself at the chosen time (e.g. open it only for the weekend).</li>\n              <li><b>Monitor whether it is up</b> — the panel tests the server's port and alerts you if it goes down (TCP only).</li>\n            </ul>\n            <h3>Status</h3>\n            <table class=\"table\">\n              <tr><td><span class=\"badge b-green\">Active</span></td><td>Port open on the MikroTik.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><span class=\"badge b-gray\">Disabled / Expired</span></td><td>Port closed (by you or by the deadline). You can re-enable it with ▶️.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><span class=\"badge b-red\">Missing on MikroTik</span></td><td>Rule deleted directly in Winbox. Use 🔁 to recreate it.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><span class=\"badge b-green\">Responding</span> / <span class=\"badge b-red\">Down</span></td><td>Result of the test on the internal server. \"Down\" = server off, program closed or a firewall blocking.</td></tr>\n            </table>\n            <p><b>⏱️</b> changes the expiration · <b>🔍 See all MikroTik rules</b> also shows forwards made outside the panel (the panel never touches them).</p>\n            <p class=\"text-amber-300\">⚠️ Only open ports you really need. Avoid exposing RDP (3389) and SSH (22) to the whole internet — prefer access through the VPN.</p>\n            <button class=\"btn\" @click=\"tab='nat'\">Go to Port forwarding →</button>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-tools\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>🛠️ Tools <span class=\"badge b-blue\">admin</span></h2>\n            <p>Tests run <b>by the MikroTik itself</b> chosen in the selector at the top — like running the command in its terminal, without opening Winbox.</p>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b>📶 Ping</b> — checks whether the router reaches an address and how long the reply takes. Good for testing a VPN client (e.g. <span class=\"mono\">10.10.0.2</span>), a server on the network (e.g. the Minecraft one) or the internet (<span class=\"mono\">8.8.8.8</span>).</li>\n              <li><b>🧭 Traceroute</b> — shows the path to the destination, hop by hop. Helps you see where the connection stops or gets slow. Hops with \"*\" didn't reply (common on ISP routers).</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p>With several routers, switch the selector to test from a client's MikroTik (e.g. does it reach the camera on its network?).</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-monitor\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>📈 Charts and usage</h2>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b>Chart</b> — choose a VPN client (device or remote MikroTik) or a forwarded port and the period (1 hour to 30 days).\n                For VPN: <b>Download</b> = what the device received; <b>Upload</b> = what it sent. \"Online X% of the time\" shows how long it stayed connected.\n                For ports: incoming traffic and the server's <b>availability</b>.</li>\n              <li><b>Usage per client</b> — how much each device used in the period and when it was last seen.</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p>History is kept for the time set in Settings (30 days by default).</p>\n            <button class=\"btn\" @click=\"tab='monitor'\">Go to Charts and usage →</button>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-events\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>🔔 Events</h2>\n            <p>Automatic timeline of the system: VPN connections and disconnections (\"VPN: …\" for devices, \"Site: …\" for clients' MikroTiks), servers that went down/came back, ports that expired, rules deleted from outside and MikroTik outages. With several routers, each event starts with its name (e.g. <span class=\"mono\">[Bakery] MikroTik unreachable</span>). Everything is recorded here, even what doesn't trigger an alert.</p>\n            <p>Colors: <span class=\"badge b-green\">success</span> something good · <span class=\"badge b-blue\">info</span> informational · <span class=\"badge b-amber\">warn</span> attention · <span class=\"badge b-red\">error</span> problem.</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-alerts\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>📣 Alerts <span class=\"badge b-blue\">admin</span></h2>\n            <p>Sends events to your phone through Discord and/or Telegram.</p>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b>Discord</b> — in the channel you want: Edit Channel → Integrations → Webhooks → New Webhook → Copy Webhook URL. Paste it into the panel and turn it on.</li>\n              <li><b>Telegram</b> — create a bot with <b>@BotFather</b> (/newbot), send a message to the bot and get the Chat ID at <span class=\"mono\">api.telegram.org/bot&lt;TOKEN&gt;/getUpdates</span>.</li>\n              <li><b>When to notify</b> — choose which events send a message. They cover all routers; each notification says which router it is about. A client router going down is only reported after it has been online once (no alert while the script hasn't been pasted yet).</li>\n              <li><b>Router notices</b> — \"RouterOS update available\" (checked once a day) and, if you want, \"MikroTik configuration changed\" (when the automatic backup keeps a new copy).</li>\n              <li><b>Language</b> — alerts and events use the language chosen in Settings → 🌐 Language.</li>\n              <li>Use <b>Save and send test</b> to confirm it arrives.</li>\n            </ul>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-seccheck\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>🛡️ Security check <span class=\"badge b-blue\">admin</span></h2>\n            <p>Checks, on the selected router, the points that most often expose a MikroTik and the panel. <b>Read-only</b>: nothing is changed. Each item shows ✅ ok, ⚠️ attention or ❌ problem, plus the command to fix it.</p>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b>Panel access</b> — warns if you opened the panel from a public IP (it must stay on the local network and the VPN).</li>\n              <li><b>Port forward to the panel</b> — looks for NAT rules that expose the panel's port to the internet.</li>\n              <li><b>Input firewall</b> — checks that the rule blocking internet access to the router exists (input chain). Without it, Winbox, WebFig, API and DNS may be open.</li>\n              <li><b>Services</b> — Telnet/FTP enabled (they send passwords unencrypted) and services that accept any address.</li>\n              <li><b>Users</b> — the default <span class=\"mono\">admin</span> user active and the <span class=\"mono\">wgpanel</span> user without an address restriction.</li>\n              <li><b>Open DNS</b> — the router's DNS answering the internet.</li>\n              <li><b>RouterOS update</b> — once a day the panel asks the router whether there is a new version (and notifies on Discord/Telegram). <b>⬆️ Check for updates</b> checks right away. To update: make a backup and, in the terminal, run <span class=\"mono\">/system package update install</span> (the router reboots).</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p class=\"hint\">The check covers the most common mistakes; it doesn't replace a full audit. The new-version notice also shows on the router cards.</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-users\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>👥 Users <span class=\"badge b-blue\">admin</span></h2>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b>Administrator</b> — can do everything: create/remove clients and ports, see QR codes, change settings.</li>\n              <li><b>Read-only</b> — sees status, charts and events, but changes nothing and cannot see QR codes/keys. Ideal for people who only need to follow along.</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p>These users belong to the panel only — they are unrelated to the MikroTik's users.</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-backup\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>💾 Backup <span class=\"badge b-blue\">admin</span></h2>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b>Download backup</b> — a file with everything in the panel: registered routers (with API passwords), interfaces, clients (with keys), remote MikroTiks, ports, settings, alerts and users. Keep it somewhere safe: whoever has the file can get into your VPN and routers.</li>\n              <li><b>Restore</b> — imports a backup (e.g. when moving the panel to another machine). Existing items are updated, nothing is deleted. What's missing is recreated only on the <b>main router</b>; items of the other routers go into the panel (they are already configured on their MikroTiks). Accepts backups from older versions.</li>\n              <li><b>🕒 Automatic MikroTik backup</b> — every day (or week) the panel exports each router's configuration (without passwords) and keeps a copy <b>only when something changed</b>, up to the chosen limit. <b>🔍 What changed</b> compares it with the previous copy (lines with + were added, with − were removed); <b>⬇️ .rsc</b> downloads the file. They are stored in the panel's <span class=\"mono\">data/</span> folder. It needs the <span class=\"mono\">ftp</span> permission on the <span class=\"mono\">wgpanel</span> user (see <a @click=\"scrollHelp('problems')\">Troubleshooting</a>).</li>\n              <li><b>MikroTik backup</b> — saves a <span class=\"mono\">.backup</span> and a <span class=\"mono\">.rsc</span> of the entire configuration of the <b>router chosen in the selector</b>, on the router itself (Winbox → Files). Do it before big changes.</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p><b>Switching panels</b> (e.g. from the PC to the container on the MikroTik):</p>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b>Everything</b> (VPN, ports, alerts, users, routers): Download backup on the old one → Restore on the new one.</li>\n              <li><b>Only the VPN</b>: on the new panel, in WireGuard VPN, click <b>Adopt and import clients</b> — with the keys stored on the router, they come with QR Codes. Alerts, users and ports you set up again.</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p>Then turn off the old panel so you don't get duplicate alerts.</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-settings\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>⚙️ Settings <span class=\"badge b-blue\">admin</span></h2>\n            <p>Items marked with <span class=\"badge b-blue\">🖧 name</span> belong to the <b>router chosen in the selector</b>; the others apply to the whole panel.</p>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b>Remote / local address</b> <span class=\"badge b-blue\">🖧</span> — the DDNS/public IP and the local IP of this router; they go into the QR codes, files and scripts created on it. Changed your DDNS? Update it here and generate the configurations again.</li>\n              <li><b>Keepalive / MTU</b> — advanced; the defaults work in most cases. (DNS and reachable networks are set per interface: VPN → ⚙️.)</li>\n              <li><b>Store the clients' private key on the MikroTik</b> — on by default. Lets any panel import the clients with QR Codes. Anyone who is admin on the MikroTik can see the keys, and they are included in the router's backups.</li>\n              <li><b>Identifying internet traffic (NAT)</b> <span class=\"badge b-blue\">🖧</span> — keep \"WAN interface list\" if your MikroTik uses the default configuration.</li>\n              <li><b>🌐 Language</b> — the language of this screen (saved in this browser; also on the login screen and in the side menu) and the language of alerts and events (for everyone).</li>\n              <li><b>WAN interface</b> <span class=\"badge b-blue\">🖧</span> — empty = detect automatically from the default route (recommended). Fill it in only to force another one.</li>\n              <li><b>Consider offline after</b> — time without a handshake before a client shows as offline. Safe minimum: 150 s (below that you get false alerts).</li>\n              <li><b>Keep history</b> — how many days to keep chart data.</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p class=\"hint\">The connection to the main MikroTik (IP, user, password, collection interval) is in the <span class=\"mono\">.env</span> file — or in the container variables, if the panel runs inside the MikroTik. The other routers' connection is in <a @click=\"scrollHelp('routers')\">Routers</a> → ✏️ Edit.</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-audit\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>📜 Audit log <span class=\"badge b-blue\">admin</span></h2>\n            <p>Record of who did what and when: logins, clients created/removed, ports opened, QR codes viewed, settings changed.</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-glossary\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>📚 Glossary</h2>\n            <dl class=\"grid sm:grid-cols-[10rem_1fr] gap-x-4 gap-y-2 text-sm\">\n              <dt>WireGuard</dt><dd>Modern, fast and secure VPN technology, built into the MikroTik (RouterOS 7).</dd>\n              <dt>Peer / Client</dt><dd>Each device (or remote MikroTik) authorized to connect to the VPN.</dd>\n              <dt>Main router</dt><dd>Your MikroTik, configured in the <span class=\"mono\">.env</span> file. The others (clients') are registered in the panel.</dd>\n              <dt>Limited user</dt><dd>The <span class=\"mono\">wgpanel</span> user the panel uses on MikroTiks: it reads and configures, but does not touch users/passwords; on clients' routers, it only logs in through the tunnel.</dd>\n              <dt>REST API</dt><dd>The RouterOS 7 \"conversation port\" the panel uses (through the www/www-ssl service). Winbox uses another protocol.</dd>\n              <dt>WireGuard interface</dt><dd>A VPN \"server\" inside the MikroTik, with its own port and IP range. You can have several.</dd>\n              <dt>Site-to-site</dt><dd>A tunnel between two whole networks (two routers), instead of a single device. It is the \"Other MikroTik\".</dd>\n              <dt>Route</dt><dd>Tells the router how to reach a network. The panel creates routes to the network of each remote MikroTik.</dd>\n              <dt>Handshake</dt><dd>A periodic \"handshake\" between the device and the MikroTik. If it happened recently, the client is connected.</dd>\n              <dt>Endpoint</dt><dd>Address (IP or DDNS + port) where the client finds the MikroTik.</dd>\n              <dt>DDNS</dt><dd>A fixed name (e.g. <span class=\"mono\">xxxx.sn.mynetname.net</span>) that always points to your IP, even when it changes.</dd>\n              <dt>NAT / Port forward</dt><dd>A rule that forwards whoever arrives from the internet on a port to a device on your network.</dd>\n              <dt>TCP / UDP</dt><dd>Types of communication. Each game/app uses one (or both) — the ready-made templates already know which.</dd>\n              <dt>WAN / LAN</dt><dd>WAN = the internet side. LAN = your internal network.</dd>\n              <dt>CGNAT</dt><dd>When your ISP doesn't give you your own public IP. It prevents opening ports and receiving VPN connections — ask your ISP for a public IP.</dd>\n              <dt>Split / Full tunnel</dt><dd>\"Local network only\" / \"All internet\" through the VPN.</dd>\n              <dt>Whitelist</dt><dd>List of allowed IPs; all others are blocked.</dd>\n              <dt>Private / public key</dt><dd>Each client's key pair. The public one stays on the MikroTik (the \"lock\"); the private one proves who the client is. It can also be stored on the MikroTik (see Settings).</dd>\n            </dl>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-about\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>💬 Ideas and support</h2>\n            <p>The panel is an open project on GitHub: <a href=\"https://github.com/ivandneto/mikrotik-wireguard-panel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">github.com/ivandneto/mikrotik-wireguard-panel</a>.</p>\n            <ul>\n              <li><b><a href=\"https://github.com/ivandneto/mikrotik-wireguard-panel/discussions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Discussions</a></b> — ideas, installation and usage questions, sharing experiences.</li>\n              <li><b><a href=\"https://github.com/ivandneto/mikrotik-wireguard-panel/issues/new/choose\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Issues</a></b> — forms to 💡 suggest a feature or 🐛 report a bug.</li>\n              <li><b><a href=\"https://github.com/ivandneto/mikrotik-wireguard-panel/releases/latest\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Releases</a></b> — new versions (Docker and container on the MikroTik) and what changed in each one.</li>\n              <li><b>Translations</b> — each language is a file in <span class=\"mono\">static/i18n/</span> (screens and manual) and <span class=\"mono\">app/locales/</span> (server messages). Corrections are welcome as a pull request.</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p class=\"text-amber-300\">⚠️ When reporting something, never paste passwords, VPN keys, the <span class=\"mono\">.env</span> file or backups. Security issues: report them privately (Security → Report a vulnerability).</p>\n            <p class=\"hint\">Panel version: <b x-text=\"'v' + (overview?.version || '?')\"></b> (also in the side menu, below \"WG Panel\"). Compare it with the <a href=\"https://github.com/ivandneto/mikrotik-wireguard-panel/releases/latest\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">latest Release</a> to know whether there is an update.<br>MIT license. Independent project, not affiliated with MikroTik.</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <div id=\"help-problems\" class=\"card space-y-3\">\n            <h2>🛠️ Troubleshooting</h2>\n            <table class=\"table\">\n              <tr><td><b>MikroTik offline</b></td><td>Check the IP and password in the <span class=\"mono\">.env</span> file, whether the <span class=\"mono\">www-ssl</span> service is enabled (IP → Services) and whether the panel's computer is on an allowed network. After editing <span class=\"mono\">.env</span>, run <span class=\"mono\">docker compose up -d</span>.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>VPN connects but reaches nothing</b></td><td>In VPN → ⚙️ of the interface, keep \"Treat the VPN as an internal network\" checked and apply again.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>VPN connected, but a device on the network won't open</b></td><td>The target device is rejecting the VPN range (10.10.0.x):<br>\n                • <b>MikroTik (WebFig/Winbox)</b>: allow it in IP → Services → <span class=\"mono\">available-from</span> (or <span class=\"mono\">address</span> on older versions) including <span class=\"mono\">10.10.0.0/24</span>.<br>\n                • <b>Windows PC</b>: Windows Firewall only accepts its own network. In PowerShell (admin): <span class=\"mono\">New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName \"VPN WireGuard\" -Direction Inbound -RemoteAddress 10.10.0.0/24 -Action Allow</span><br>\n                • <b>Use the IP</b>, not the name (<span class=\"mono\">\\\\192.168.88.61</span> instead of <span class=\"mono\">\\\\my-pc</span>). Apps that \"discover devices on the network\" automatically don't work over the VPN.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>New/reinstalled panel without the clients</b></td><td>In WireGuard VPN you'll see \"This MikroTik already has WireGuard\": click <b>Adopt and import clients</b>. Or, to bring everything (alerts, users, routers), use <b>Backup → Restore</b> with a backup of the old panel.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Client router shows \"No connection\" in Routers</b></td><td>Its tunnel (VPN) must be Online. Check that the management part of the script was pasted and use <b>🔌 Test</b>, which looks for the API port. If their www only accepts some IPs, the script tells you: add the tunnel range in IP → Services → www.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Client's MikroTik won't go Online</b></td><td>Check on the client's router whether the script ran without errors (<span class=\"mono\">/interface wireguard print</span> should show <span class=\"mono\">wg-central</span>), whether it has internet and whether your remote address (DDNS) is right in Settings. The interface's UDP port must be open on your MikroTik (the panel does that when creating the interface).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Tunnel Online, but the client's network won't open</b></td><td>Check that the network entered for the client is right (✏️) and that the device there accepts connections from another network (Windows firewall, cameras with restrictions). If the client's MikroTik doesn't use the default LAN list, create an accept rule on it for the <span class=\"mono\">wg-central</span> interface.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>\"Conflicts with your network\" when creating Other MikroTik</b></td><td>The client's network is the same as (or overlaps) yours or another client's. Change the client's LAN range (e.g. 192.168.10.0/24) before creating the tunnel.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Peer removed in Winbox</b></td><td>The panel shows \"Missing on MikroTik\". Use 🔁 to recreate it or 🗑️ in the panel to remove it for good — removing it through the panel also cleans up its routes and rules.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>I updated the panel and don't see the change</b></td><td>Check the version in the side menu (below \"WG Panel\") and reload the page. If the panel runs on the MikroTik, replace the container image with the new version's <span class=\"mono\">wgpanel-mikrotik-arm64-vX.Y.Z.tar</span> file (see the README); <span class=\"mono\">/container print</span> shows the version in the comment.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>VPN works at home, but not away</b></td><td>Use the <b>Remote access</b> QR away from home. Check that the remote address (DDNS) is right in Settings.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Client shows Offline but is connected</b></td><td>Normal if it sits idle for a few minutes with keepalive turned off. With the default (25s) it doesn't happen.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Forwarded port doesn't work</b></td><td>The server's IP changed (make the lease static), the server's firewall is blocking, the program is closed, or you tested from inside your own network (test over 4G).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>\"Down\" on the service</b></td><td>The panel could not connect to the internal server: it is off, the program isn't running or the internal port is wrong.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>\"Needs the 'ftp' permission\"</b> (automatic backup or update check)</td><td>Panels installed before version 1.3 created the <span class=\"mono\">wgpanel</span> user without file access. In the MikroTik terminal, as admin: <span class=\"mono\">/user group set [find name=wgpanel] policy=read,write,api,rest-api,test,sensitive,ftp</span>. The \"ftp\" permission only grants file access; it does not enable the FTP service.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>I forgot the panel password</b></td><td>Another admin can reset it in Users. With no admin at all: delete the <span class=\"mono\">data/</span> folder (you lose the panel data — make a backup first) and restart.</td></tr>\n            </table>\n          </div>\n";
